---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { md } from '@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/md.js';
import BranchRivers from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/BranchRivers.astro";
import { ledger } from '@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/lines.js';
import lines from '../data/lines.json';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const u = (h) => base + h;
/* The lists of people under each figure (the lanes' members) read names, not slugs. */
const names = Object.fromEntries(register.people.filter((p) => p.slug).map((p) => [p.slug, p.name]));

/* THE FIGURE'S TWO TEXTS. The dek is printed once, by the paragraph above each figure; it was also being
   handed to the figure as its caption, so it appeared twice under every heading, and the page's own
   explanation of the figure sat in a second caption beneath that. The explanation is the figure's caption now.
   The accessible title carries no number and no surname: both figures are given this one text, so
   a title that said "8 lanes" or named LEIBHOLZ would be read aloud over the LUWINSKI figure too. This one
   is true of both. (The kit now gives each figure its own <title> id and points that figure's
   aria-labelledby at it, so each figure announces its own title; check:lines checks the pairing.) */
const FIGURE_TITLE = 'The lanes of one surname, each kept apart, and the road each of them took';
const FIGURE_CAPTION = `Hover or tab any dot and it says what the document behind it is. The top lane of each
          diagram is the one this archive descends from. <strong>A dashed band is a family of the
          same name that is documented and is definitely <em>not</em> ours</strong> &mdash; drawn
          here so that the next person who finds the name does not have to make the mistake again.`;

/* 21 September 2026. The lanes are the kit's figure now. Three archives drew
   this by hand — Defranceski's five families arriving in Istria, Falco's five
   strands of one surname, and this one's lanes — and Falco's own component
   said outright that it was copying Defranceski's grammar. The geometry that
   used to live here, and the two hundred units of it that differed from the
   other two for no reason, are gone.

   WHAT DID NOT MOVE is the argument, which is this archive's alone: a rare
   name plus a shared address is the shape of the mistake this archive has
   already made twice, so each household gets a lane and nothing is merged
   until a document joins it. The two footers under each figure carry that —
   households of the name not yet on any lane, and names attached to no lane
   at all — and they stay on the page, because a figure that quietly dropped
   them would be claiming the lanes are the whole population.

   The component is BranchRivers, which the kit has had since it was promoted
   out of Defranceschi and which this archive's data already fitted exactly:
   lanes of { colour, label, conf, nodes, ends }, with unplaced and loose
   underneath. No adapter was needed at all, which is the clearest sign this
   figure should never have been written a third time.

   25 OCTOBER 2026 (kit 341af33): THE PENS GO TO THE FIGURE. The two footers used to be passed empty and listed
   again by this page, because the figure could not hold their sentences. It can now: each is a pen with a note
   printed under its heading and a count derived from the data, and the figure lists the people on each lane and
   in each pen under the drawing. So the page no longer prints the lists a second time, and the line that
   counted lanes and households by hand is the kit's ledger(), which cannot go stale. Each lane carries its
   own status (documented, or origin unknown) and the years the evidence reaches; a status here is the
   archive's own word, taken from what each lane said before and from the page that argues it. */
---
<Base title="The lines of the name"
      dek="Every family of Leibholz and of Luwinski this archive has met, kept in separate lanes until something joins them.">

  <p class="lede">
    They are not one family, and most of them never were. This archive keeps meeting households of
    these two surnames in the same city, in the same decade, sometimes in the same street — and a
    rare name plus a shared address is the exact shape of the mistake it has already made twice.
    <strong>So each one gets a lane of its own.</strong> What is documented goes on the lane. What
    would have to be found to join it to ours is written underneath. Nothing is merged until that
    thing is found, and nothing is thrown away either.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One spelling is not a search <span class="tag doc">The rule this page runs on</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>LEIBHOLZ · LAIBHOLZ · LEIBBHOLZ · LEIBHOLTZ · LEIPHOLZ</strong><br />
      <strong>LUWINSKI · LUWINSKY · LUWENSKI · LUWWINSKY · LOWINSKI · LOWINSKY · JUWINSKI</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      On 20 September 2026 the East Prussian index was searched on the head form alone and returned
      one woman in one village. Searched on the list, one form at a time, the <em>same index</em>
      returned a household in Sauerbaum&rsquo;s own civil district and a possible father at
      Allenstein. Two of the three best finds of that day were not under the spelling anybody would
      have typed first.
    </p>
  </div>

  {lines.sets.map((set) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{set.name}</h2>
      <p class="small muted" set:html={md(set.dek)}></p>
      <figure class="rivers">
        <BranchRivers rivers={set} title={FIGURE_TITLE} caption={FIGURE_CAPTION} names={names} />
      </figure>
      <p class="small muted ledger">
        {ledger(set).lanes} lanes drawn above &middot; {ledger(set).households} households of the name not yet
        on any lane &middot; {ledger(set).otherStock} names attached to no lane &middot; {ledger(set).peopleOnLanes}
        people linked to a lane.
      </p>

    </section>
  ))}

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What each lane owes <span class="tag inf">The standing instruction</span></h3>
    <p>
      When a new household of either name is met it gets a lane <em>before</em> it gets a paragraph
      anywhere else, and the lane carries three things: <strong>what is documented</strong>,
      <strong>what is inferred</strong>, and <strong>what would have to be found to join it</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      A lane with nothing in the third column is a lane this archive has stopped thinking about.
      There are none of those yet. The full text of every lane, with its evidence, is in
      <a href={u('/searched/')}>the evidence layer</a> under <em>The lines of the name</em>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="small"><a href={u('/worklist/')}>The work list &rarr;</a></p>

</Base>

<style>
  .rivers { margin: 1.2rem 0 1.6rem; }
  .rivers svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .lane-label { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 700; }
  .lane-conf  { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 9.5px; letter-spacing: .06em; fill: var(--ink-3); }
  .lane-read  { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11.5px; }
  .place { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600; fill: var(--ink); text-anchor: middle; }
  .year  { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; text-anchor: middle; opacity: .85; }
  .endlab { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12px; fill: var(--ink-2); }
  .endlab.soft { fill: var(--ink-3); font-style: italic; }
  .rnode { cursor: default; }
  .rnode:hover .place, .rnode:hover .endlab { fill: var(--accent); }
</style>
